---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import type { Mode, ResolvedInterlude } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { interlude: ResolvedInterlude; mode: Mode }
const { interlude, mode } = Astro.props;
if (interlude.id !== "bengio-fixed-window-prerequisite") throw new Error("Unexpected fixed-window prerequisite.");
const expert = mode === "expert";
const math = (tex: string) => renderToString(tex, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const features = math(String.raw`x=(C(w_{t-1}),C(w_{t-2}),\ldots,C(w_{t-n+1}))`);
const scores = math(String.raw`y=b+Wx+U\tanh(d+Hx)`);
const probability = math(String.raw`\hat P(w_t=i\mid w_{t-1},\ldots,w_{t-n+1})=\frac{e^{y_i}}{\sum_{k\in V}e^{y_k}}`);
---

<section class="reading-block word-prerequisite" aria-labelledby={interlude.id}>
  <p class="kicker">Prerequisite · Bengio et al. · 2000/2003</p>
  <h2 id={interlude.id}>{interlude.title}</h2>
  <p>A neural word predictor can already assign probabilities without carrying a recurrent state. Read the evaluated JMLR model as four steps.</p>
  <ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Bengio fixed-window prediction path">
    <li><strong>Window</strong><span>Select the preceding n−1 words. Earlier words are outside this explicit input.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Look up</strong><span>Retrieve each word type’s learned row from C and concatenate the rows. With weights fixed, the same word has the same lookup row in every history.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Score</strong><span>A feed-forward network turns those rows into one score per vocabulary word. The optional direct path bypasses the tanh hidden layer.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Normalize</strong><span>Softmax converts the scores into positive next-word probabilities that sum to one.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="mt-3"><strong>Two different objects:</strong> Bengio’s lookup row represents a word type; Elman’s current hidden state depends on token history. A fixed word row does not make the whole feed-forward prediction context independent: the other words in the window still affect it.</p>
  {expert && <>
    <div class="equation-panel mt-4" role="region" aria-label="Bengio lookup and score equations" tabindex="0"><div set:html={features} /><div set:html={scores} /></div>
    <p class="mt-3">C is |V| × m; x has (n−1)m entries. H maps x to h hidden units, d is their bias, U maps hidden activity to vocabulary scores, and b is the output bias. W is the optional direct word-feature-to-score path; W = 0 removes it. These are the journal’s symbols, not Mikolov’s weight matrices.</p>
    <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Bengio normalized next-word probability" tabindex="0"><div set:html={probability} /></div>
    <p class="mt-3">Here y denotes <em>scores</em>. In Mikolov’s notation below, y denotes the already normalized <em>probabilities</em>. Equation 1 appears on printed p. 1143; the lookup and softmax definitions span pp. 1141–1143.</p>
  </>}
  {interlude.blocks.map((block) => <div class="mt-4"><h3 id={block.id}>{block.heading}</h3><p class="mt-3">{block.body}</p>{block.evidence.map((evidence) => <EvidenceDetails evidence={evidence} />)}</div>)}
  <p class="provenance-note mt-4">The three-author NIPS 2000 paper and four-author JMLR 2003 article form an editorial version family. This prerequisite uses the journal architecture and locators; their datasets and results remain separate. Mikolov cites the JMLR article, although its reference omits Jauvin.</p>
  <a class="claim-link mt-3" href="/reference/works/bengio-2000-neural-probabilistic-language-model/">Inspect Bengio’s versioned work record</a>
</section>
